<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ravi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ravi is one of the five rivers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, flowing from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> on its banks. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chenab">Chenab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beas">Beas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a>, it sustained intensive cultivation of wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a>. This fertile agrarian base supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Sikh Empire governed one of the most agriculturally productive regions of the subcontinent, with the five rivers of the Punjab, the Jhelum, Chenab, <b>Ravi</b>, Beas and Sutlej, sustaining intensive cultivation of wheat, cotton and sugarcane across a densely populated agrarian hinterland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In his later years he settled at Kartarpur on the <b>Ravi</b> river, where a community of followers lived by his teaching, farming, praying and eating together.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…five were true cities: Mohenjo-daro on the Indus in Sindh, which covered some 250 hectares; Harappa on the <b>Ravi</b> in Punjab; Ganweriwala in the Cholistan desert, still largely unexcavated; Rakhigarhi in Haryana; and Dholavira on an island in the salt flats of the Rann of Kutch in Gujarat.</blockquote><a href="/culture/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Wars among the Vedic tribes also appear, above all the Battle of the Ten Kings on the river Parushni (the <b>Ravi</b>), where the Bharatas under their chief Sudas defeated a confederation of ten tribes.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vedic-civilisation">Vedic civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Coins show that by the 2nd and 1st centuries BCE they formed a state in the region between the <b>Ravi</b> and the Beas, around modern Pathankot and the Kangra valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/audumbara">Audumbara</a></li><li><blockquote>Raja Bhupat Pal is credited with founding the town of Basohli on the <b>Ravi</b> as his capital in the 1630s.</blockquote><a href="/state/basohli-state">Basohli State</a></li></ul></section>
